    yes, I’ve faced performance issues in the applications I worked on, particularly with API latency

    one example was ab API that was taking significantly longer than expected, I started by check  API response time and then traced the request flow to identity where latency was coming from ,

    during the analysis, I found few issues

    some database queries were slow because required indexing was missing
    we were frequently accessing the same data where there is no caching mechanism in place
    some API were returning complete database instead of pagination where resulted in large database queries 
what kind of caching have you used? inmemory vs distributed?
    we have used both  in-memory and distributed caching depending on the use case

    for in-memory cache, cached data is stored inside the application’s memory, it can be very fast because there is no network call

    we generally use in-memory cache when data is  relatively small, frequently accessed data, can tolerate some staleness

    for example, we used in-memory to store configuration data or reference data
    the limitation with in-memory is that cached data is not automatically synchronised with all instances, for example instance 1 updates its data, instance 2 might still hold the old data



